  "notes": {
    "P": "Optimistic bound, not certified: K1 minus every bit witnessed varying in any legal-envelope run. A certified program-only constant set from the same engine would be a subset of P (assuming it certifies nothing outside K1 and mapping is monotone), so each increment over P is a lower bound.",
    "equal_runtime": "Each row is C1 with flip-flops that are constant in its deployment replaced by their constants, so in that deployment it produces A0's outputs in the same cycles (certified by ternary reachability relative to C1 for T3, T2, T1, G12, and G2; simulation evidence only for P; C1 under its instruction-domain condition). Runtime is the deployment's: 805 cycles for the program at the native timing (fetch gap 1, data latency 3). Data latencies 2 and 1 were not timed.",
    "data_latency": "LEN5's memory emulator replies after 1 + PIPE_NUM cycles with its data answer register skipped (this study's platform: PIPE_NUM=2, three cycles). Latencies 2 and 1 need PIPE_NUM 1 or 0, which also shortens instruction responses; T2 and T1 keep the native instruction pipeline, so they model legal interface timings, not an emulator setting.",
    "G2": "Fetch gap 2 is outside LEN5's fetch contract (a refusal can lose a redirect) and the program takes 931 cycles instead of 805.",
    "C1": "Conditionally certified under the instruction-domain assumption; see isa_baseline/evidence/final."
  },
